If you perform a minimal boot, like you've done:

    sysboot> set startup_p1 "MIN"

You will need to autoconfigure other devices, such as disks and network
cards, when the system comes back up:

    $ MCR SYSMAN IO AUTOCONFIGURE


This step:

   $backup/image dqa0: dka100: 

did not install VMS on DKA100. It just made it a copy of the CD
that was in dqa0, presumably the OpenVMS 7.1-2 installation CD.

To install VMS, you should boot from the OpenVMS 7.1-2 installation CD,
which should produce a menu something like this:

        1)  Install or upgrade OpenVMS Alpha Version V7.2
        2)  Display products and patches that this procedure
            can install
        3)  Install or upgrade layered products and patches
        4)  Show installed products
        5)  Reconfigure installed products
        6)  Remove installed products
        7)  Execute DCL commands and procedures
        8)  Shut down this system

You should select option 1. You may want to review the documentation:

  http://www.openvms.digital.com:8000/72final/6522/6522pro.html
  OpenVMS Alpha Version 7.2 Upgrade and Installation Manual

IIRC, the 7.1-2 version should be on the installation CD you have.
If not, the 7.2 version will be close enough.

Please peruse the OpenVMS FAQ:

   http://www.openvms.digital.com/wizard/openvms_faq.html 

Which states

  HTML format on-line product documentation sets for specific Compaq
  OpenVMS products are presently available at:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com:8000/
    http://www.openvms.digital.com/doc/
    http://www.openvms.digital.com/commercial/

The comp.os.vms newsgroup would be a better forum for any further
questions you might have.

--Jerry Leslie     (my opinions are strictly my own)



