cdl@proxima.ucsd.edu (Carl Lowenstein) wrote:
> 
> In <8kiag7$2ve$1@mailint03.im.hou.compaq.com> Hoff Hoffman wrote:
> | 
> | In article <YX8a5$CdG@c400>, aw@mail1.bet1.puv.fi writes:
> | :Has anyone ever successfully used an Ultra2 (LVD) drive
> | :with a VAXstation 4000 (model 60)?
> | 
> |   Mixing Ultra and non-Ultra disks can be a little difficult to get
> |   correct, and I'm not particularly aware of anyone locally that has 
> |   tried this combination.  You will have to select a unit number from 
> |   the narrow space, as (IIRC) the integrated SCSI controller on this 
> |   box is narrow (zero through seven, inclusive).  I will assume that
> |   this drive is external, as some of the faster drives may not have 
> |   the cooling necessary within this enclosure, and the disks could 
> |   thus conceivably bake themselves over time...
> 
> Let me point out a couple of things.  First, SCSI is in general backwards 
> compatible.  The drive and controller negotiate to find the greatest 
> capability that they have in common.  In particular, LVD drives will revert 
> to normal single-ended SCSI in the presence of other single-ended equipment, 
> namely the controller, and will transfer data as fast as the controller can 
> accept it.
> 
> Second, there has been a great improvement in disk drive power efficiency 
> over the past few years.  Don't look at the drive speed, look at its power 
> dissipation.  The drives that came with the VaxStation were probably 
> dissipating about 10 to 12 watts.  Modern 7200rpm drives dissipate 10 to 12 
> watts.
> 
>     carl
