Just finished scanning Terry's Australia presentation (FinalOzTug.ppt,
downloaded from acersoft.com).

Granting that he was presenting to a Tandem audience, it sure looked like he
had a far more encouraging story to tell Tandem users than he would have had
to tell VMS users:  prognostications of an explosion of Himalaya sales in
the enterprise space, reports of vigorous R&D efforts, lists of Tandem
strongholds that included most of those that Compaq lists as VMS
strongholds...

It would not be a stretch to suspect that the reason we haven't seen more
enthusiasm from Compaq (save for verbal reassurances and efforts involving
minimal funding) regarding its 'VMS renaissance' is not because they have
the combined intelligence of a tree-stump but because they have alternate
plans that just don't include VMS as an important contributor:  Tandem in
the enterprise space, Tru64 in the mid-range (including its developing
clustering abilities and its NonStop configuration on the Tandem Integrity
hardware - RIP ftVAX), and Windows on IA hardware wherever they can sell it.
And given that we already know that Compaq likes to avoid competing with
itself, it's hard to find a significant hole for VMS to fill in such a
strategy, even though we could suggest that a different strategy that
included a significant VMS presence (and more product overlap) might be a
stronger one overall.

That would explain Compaq's actions (right up to the present) a lot better
than postulating a continuing suicidal dependence on eventual Windows
supremacy.  It would also be a lot more difficult to argue against, since
they may well be willing to compromise ultimate market share if it allows
them to simplify their product set (as I've said from the first, I really
don't think they're comfortable being a software developer).

Comments?

- bill






