This depends critically upon the application that you want to "scale".  If
the application is sufficiently parallel that it runs well on a Beowulf
style machine it will scale very well indeed on Linux - and the cost per
cpu will be a fraction of what it would be on an equivalent SMP system.
Obviously I don't have access to the real numbers, but judging from the 
number of sites I've read about with hundreds of single CPU alphas engaged 
in this sort of activity I can estimate that the fraction of Alpha CPUs
used for massively parallel computing is at least as high as that currently
utilized in SMP machines. 

