In article <sn06icdrnd681@corp.supernews.com>, "Jonathan H. Watmuff" <watmuff@pop.netgate.net> writes:
:The "a" series appear to be more widely available, but I  don't want to 
:waste my money on a system that cannot be converted.

  Unless you want to play around with the hardware as part of the hobbyist
  experience, consider getting yourself an -au series box or an AlphaStation 
  series system.  

  Some of the "whitebox" (Windows NT only) systems can be converted over 
  to SRM and to OpenVMS hobbyist use, and some (eg: XL) cannot.  Even some 
  of the "whitebox" systems -- once switched over to SRM -- can have their 
  share of problems.  (I've had more than a few folks hit the Intel SIO IDE 
  CD-ROM bootstrap limitation in the past few weeks, so much so that I'm 
  adding it into the OpenVMS FAQ.)

  The whole discussion around ensuring the configuration contains only
  supported controllers (particularly graphics and the storage I/O PCI
  SCSI or IDE widgets) for the core OpenVMS bootstrap functions is another 
  entire issue, of course...

  I personally particularly like some of the features that are available 
  on the AlphaStation series, such as the failsafe loader -- if you toast 
  the firmware on various members of the the AlphaStation series, you can 
  manually trigger a ROM-based loader to reload new firmware.  (Without
  access to a failsafe loader, you can end up needing specialized hardware 
  to reload (toasted) firmware.)  EV45 300 MHz or EV5 or later AlphaStation
  systems tend to make nice hobbyist systems, assuming that sufficient 
  physical memory is configured.  These systems are old enough that they
  are rather inexpensive on the used-equipment market, and most of (all 
  of?) the AlphaStation series is directly supported by OpenVMS.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




