        We have an old VAXstation 3100/38 running VMS 5.5-2 (I think, it
    is definitely some  variation  of  V5.5).   It  is  running a custom
    application  for which uses a Kinetic Systems SCSI-CAMAC  controller
    with the vendor-supplied SCSI device driver.  Because the author  of
    the  application  has retired, and because it is not clear what work
    would need to be done to get  the device driver running under a more
    recent  version  of  VMS  (let alone, Alpha/VMS),  we're  not  in  a
    position to upgrade the O/S.

        Amazingly enough, this stand-alone system which runs pretty much
    7x24 and mostly unattended, has _no_ removable storage!  In order to
    capture a snapshot of its two disks,  I  had to add it to my cluster
    last summer and do backups to my disks (and thence to tape).

        Therfore, I'd like  recommendations  for  a  tape drive for this
    system.   The  requirements are that VMS 5.5 support the  drive  and
    that I can build (and boot and run!) STABACKIT on that drive.

        My first thought was an 8mm  drive, which would probably need to
    be  an older 8200 variety.  But I can't recall whether STABACKIT can
    be built on 8mm drives.  And personally, I  don't  like  8mm  drives
    much at all...

        Thinking about it, I'd probably prefer  something like a TZ86 or
    TZ87.   Does VMS 5.5 support these drives, and can I get them  (used
    equipment  certainly)  in  table-top  enclosures?   What  about  4mm
    drives?

        Thanks, Ken
