Date: 2000/07/14
Message-ID: <rdeininger-1407002357080001@user-2ivebgl.dialup.mindspring.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 646580915
References: <$EPNU4c0TUGQ@mccdev.slac.stanford.edu>
Organization: MindSpring Enterprises
X-Server-Date: 15 Jul 2000 03:56:14 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec

In article <$EPNU4c0TUGQ@mccdev.slac.stanford.edu>, Fairfield@SLAC.Stanford.EDU (Ken Fairfield; SLAC: 650-926-2924; FAX: 926-3515) wrote:

>         We have an old VAXstation 3100/38 running VMS 5.5-2 (I think, it
>     is definitely some  variation  of  V5.5).   It  is  running a custom
>     application  for which uses a Kinetic Systems SCSI-CAMAC  controller
>     with the vendor-supplied SCSI device driver.  Because the author  of
>     the  application  has retired, and because it is not clear what work
>     would need to be done to get  the device driver running under a more
>     recent  version  of  VMS  (let alone, Alpha/VMS),  we're  not  in  a
>     position to upgrade the O/S.

You should perhaps revisit this choice.  We have a microvax 3500 (Q-bus)
talking to CAMAC though an MBD, with a locally-repaired descendent of 
very buggy LAMPF Q device driver.  Our driver probably has nothing in
common with yours, so take our experience with a grain of salt.  When
we went from 5.5-2 to 7.1, I'm pretty sure the driver didn't need any
changes.  We definitely re-assembled it, which I think is a documented
requirement for new OS versions.  Assuming you have the source code, or
can pry it out of Kinetic Systems, I suggest you TRY the driver on a newer version of VMS.  At the very least, ask KS to rebuild the driver for you
on VMS 7.1.  Try it on a spare system before you clobber the existing one.
(3100s show up on ebay a lot.)

Device drivers are designed to not break across OS versions, provided they
follow the documented rules.  I think there's a very good chance it would
work without much trouble.  There were some changes required before 5.5-2, but once you got a driver that far, it seemed to stay happy.

The application that calls the driver  might not even need to be re-linked.  Your milage may vary.

7.1 would give you LOTs more tape drive options.  Generic SCSI device 
support is pretty good.
 
>         Amazingly enough, this stand-alone system which runs pretty much
>     7x24 and mostly unattended, has _no_ removable storage!

Which part is amazing?  Not the 7x24 unattended, surely.  And the 3100
was pretty low-end when new, and there were no low-end tape drives back then.
(You could get a 3100 with a floppy drive, but that hardly counts.)  So this sounds like a normal situation, not amazing. :-)

>     In order to
>     capture a snapshot of its two disks,  I  had to add it to my cluster
>     last summer and do backups to my disks (and thence to tape).

Not a bad solution.  If it's not too often, this seems fine.  Unless the network police have cut off your MOP and SCS traffic to this poor old system.
 
>         Therfore, I'd like  recommendations  for  a  tape drive for this
>     system.   The  requirements are that VMS 5.5 support the  drive  and
>     that I can build (and boot and run!) STABACKIT on that drive.

Check the exact OS version.  There is quite a lot of difference between
5.5 and 5.5-2.
 
>         My first thought was an 8mm  drive, which would probably need to
>     be  an older 8200 variety.  But I can't recall whether STABACKIT can
>     be built on 8mm drives.  And personally, I  don't  like  8mm  drives
>     much at all...

I don't know if we could boot from an exabyte 8200 on our system, but we
have a very peculiar SCSI interface.  You'll probably have to try it.  Surely
someone can provide an 8200 for a test?  Nobody covets those damn beasts.
But I agree with you, an 8mm should be last resort.  4mm DAT may be a bit
less flakey, but I've not used them.  Any sort of DLT-class drive would
be far superior.

I believe there was once a DEC-labeled exabyte of the 8200 class.  If anything
would boot, that would be the most likely.  Might be hard to find, though.


>         Thinking about it, I'd probably prefer  something like a TZ86 or
>     TZ87.   Does VMS 5.5 support these drives, and can I get them  (used
>     equipment  certainly)  in  table-top  enclosures?   What  about  4mm
>     drives?

Dunno.  We skipped from 8200 drives to DLT 4000, so I have no experience
with TZ8x.  5.5 may be too old.  Getting one to fit into an enclosure should
be easy.  As a last resort, buy a DLT enclosure.

-- 
Robert Deininger
