The VAXstation 2000 has a power supply that requires a certain load in
order for it to function properly.  Trying to power it up without a disk
drive generally causes the machine to either act flakey or to not come up
at all.  This'll be a seperate problem from the console cable thing.

The diskless VS2Ks sold by DEC actually came with a hefty resistor board
that plugged into the power connector for the disk drive.  When lacking
this resistor board, I've just used junk half-height or full-height 5.25in
MFM drives to create the proper load. 

With the current snapshot of NetBSD/vax (1.5_Alpha), both the MFM and SCSI
interfaces on the VS2K are supported.  You can't boot from SCSI disks
attached to the VS2K SCSI interface, but it would be possible to bootstrap
from either floppy, an MFM HD, or the network and then have a SCSI drive
get mounted up as the system disk.  

