In article <8k2ml5$fuf$1@sword.avalon.net>,
Jeffrey Dunitz <orpheus@arthur.avalon.net> wrote:
>bdc@world.std.com (Brian Chase) writes:

>Ah. I tried several different combinations of cables and terminal
>settings with the terminal plugged into the printer port. Still nothing.
>Should I assume this machine is dead?

Looks like Roger Ivie already said it, but I'd focus on the cable some
more.   Make sure you're using the DEC pinouts and not the IBM PC style
9-pin pinouts.  Assuming that for some reason the printer serial port is
dead, you might try finding one of the monochrome DEC monitors and see if
there's any signs of life on the graphic console.  

If you then can't get anything on the graphic console, you might be
looking at a dead system.  My next step after that would be to pull out
the VS2K system board out to make sure all the chips are seated well. 
It's approaching voodoo at that point, but more than once I've taken
systems apart and reassembled them only to have a problem magically
disappear. 

>>With the current snapshot of NetBSD/vax (1.5_Alpha), both the MFM and SCSI
>>interfaces on the VS2K are supported.  You can't boot from SCSI disks
>>attached to the VS2K SCSI interface, but it would be possible to bootstrap
>>from either floppy, an MFM HD, or the network and then have a SCSI drive
>>get mounted up as the system disk.  
>
>Any MFM disk, or does it have to be exactly one of the three sold by DEC?
>I saw a thing about how to calculate some stuff to make other disks work.
>Is that necessary if the disk I put in is the same size as a real DEC disk?

It is my understanding that any MFM disk formatted with the VS2K ROM
formatter will work with the VS2K.  The main thing would be getting the
format parameters right.  Documentation exists on the net for the
parameters of those MFM drives which DEC OEMed.  I recall someone doing a
generic overview of how to figure the parameters for any MFM drive, but I
can't seem to find it just now. 

-brian.
