In article <DTiotGxQ0bj6-pn2-tbt9hMs2D6GS@localhost>, djweath@attglobal.net (Dave Weatherall) writes...
}On Sun, 6 Jul 3900 01:21:49, Robert Alan Byer 
}<byer@mail.ourservers.net> wrote:
} 
}> Again, currently only the Alpha platform is supported with MegaPOV
}> because it requires the IEE_FLOAT mode which VAX dosen't have.
} 
}Just out of curiosity, why is IEEE_FLOAT a necessity? What's wrong 
}with G_, F_, D_floatings?
} 
}Cheers - Dave.

Blame the person who wrote the radiosity related stuff - as far as I could
tell when I did a port a few years back, that was the only part that required
IEEE floats. Why does it require them? Because it does direct bitwise
operations on the floating point data. I have no idea why. Everybody I
have mentioned this to (including at least one POVray developer) agreees
that this is a really bad thing to do.

In theory, you should be able to build POV on a VAX and have everything work
except radiosity. You could either trim that out of the program, or just build
it and expect trouble if you ever try to use the radiosity.

Note that this is for just plain old POV. I havn't checked the MeagaPOV
updates. It is posisble that they do something similar to the stupid thing
that the radiosity code does.

--- Carl



