Terry C. Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> wrote in message
news:FxC1ut.4uq@world.std.com...
> Compaq today posted the third issue of its OpenVMS TIMES customer
> newsletter. Volume 1 Number 3 is a 960KB PDF that can be downloaded from
>
> http://www.openvms.digital.com/openvmstimes/

Busy couple of days, but I finally got around to reading it - so here's my
usual reality-check response:

Capellas pigeon-holed VMS as 'by definition' operating in mission-critical
situations in his so-called interview (I mean, *I* understand the difference
between a *real* interview and a staged conversation with soft-ball
questions and set-up answers, and I assume most of the rest of you do as
well, so why insult our intelligence?).  While it's nice to emphasize VMS's
strengths in this area, a Compaq strategy to sell VMS *only* when nothing
else will do the job is not going to give it the 'renaissance' that a lot of
us might hope for - and if Capellas actually sees VMS the way he described
it, then we can't hope for much help from him in this regard.

"I think [VMS is] almost uniquely qualified to service the demands of
e-business," said Capellas, conveniently ignoring the fact that while VMS
indeed provides in many cases uniquely superior under-pinnings, a lot of the
super-structure required by e-business is available only on more popular
platforms, a situation which even with major efforts by Compaq won't change
immediately - assuming that such efforts are in fact made at all.

"Now it's a question of taking the fight to the streets and taking our
message to the market" would be a more stirring phrase if I hadn't heard it
(plus a couple of other statements in the article) *verbatim* a month ago
from a different source in the same context:  at least one of them was
offering someone else's words as his own (OK, call me naive:  I still don't
approve of it).

I'd have a lot more faith in these guys if their public demeanor didn't
remind me so strongly of politicians or used-car salesmen far more
interested in making a sale than in satisfying a need.  Until Compaq
understands what customers need and tries to provide it, I fear they will
continue to get clobbered by other companies more in tune with reality:
enterprise computing is serious business, and purchase decisions are made
accordingly.  It would also certainly not have hurt to have had someone who
understands that Galaxy has *nothing* to do with (heterogeneous)
inter-operability proof-read the article to avoid that particular
embarrassment.

Moving right along, I suppose I shouldn't be any less exacting in
criticising Terry's article:

"Compaq senior management clearly regards OpenVMS as a valuable strategic
asset, not a cash cow that's destined for a one-way trip to the abbatoir."
'Clearly'?  Perhaps when we see evidence of a real advertising campaign
(instead of a couple of twitches indicating that what we thought was a
long-dead corpse might have a breath of life hiding away somewhere), real
willingness to compete with other Compaq systems, and real development
commitments aimed at widening the long-narrowing gap between VMS and its
inferior but faster-moving competitors, *then* there will be 'clear'
evidence to that effect.  Meanwhile, sounds more like clear evidence of a
successful snow job.

And "... it now appears that OpenVMS is poised for a renaissance and perhaps
even a return to growth mode" - ?  Any definition of 'renaissance' in this
context that did *not* minimally imply a 'return to growth mode' would be a
rather strange one, IMO.

Terry's got a somewhat fine line to walk, especially in a Compaq
publication:  it's not wise to alienate your sources, and he's naturally
enthusiastic after all his years with VMS to see a potential turn-around
occurring.  But there's a taint of fluff in this piece (accompanying a fair
amount of non-fluff, I hasten to add) that won't enhance his reputation for
objectivity (though since, come to think of it, this may be a job
requirement for anyone claiming to be an 'industry analyst', it may be
unfair to single him out).

Ah, well.  The rest of the publication seemed mildly informative or at worst
innocuous.  Perhaps one should expect no better from something which is
effectively a sales brochure - except in those areas, like the first two
articles, where it pretends to be something more substantial.

- bill






