    I'm told that the 2100's and 3100's have a NiCad battery pack, so if the
machine is unplugged it will eventually discharge, but having it on and
running will recharge it.

> Does the DECStation 3100 require a functioning network connection before

    No, it's just that the path to the boot drive is lost when the NVRAM
goes, the system doesn't look for it, it only goes where you tell it to. I
believe scsi pb is the command to look for SCSI devices attached, and then
setenv bootpath rz(0,X,0)bootfile and then typing boot should get it going.
(Where X is the SCSI ID of the HD and bootfile is the name of the kernel you
want to boot.
    You might check http://www.netbsd.org/Ports/pmax and see if there is any
info in the FAQ on the ROM commands and what they mean. If all else, try
typing ? or help at the >>> and it should get you started.

    Chris




