:>> I suspect most of the DS series required external disks.  And of course
:>> there has to be a disk at SCSI ID=3 with a bootable system on it.

Well, not most, but larger machines like the 5000/2XX used nearly all
of their internal space for RAM.

:>I opened it up this morning and there are two hard disk in it - an RZ24E
:>and a RZ23L. Interestingly, neither disk seems to have any SCSI jumpers
:>fitted and neither spins up!
:> 
:>> I think that is the problem... maybe the disk will no longer spin up.
:>> Maybe it is no longer readable.  Listen to the system when it powers on
:>> and see if you can hear a disk winding up to speed, then listen for the
:>> head load, then listen as the unit tries to boot.

:>Both disks appear to be dead so I'm investigating the PSU with a
:>voltmeter now.

Well, they might just wait for the command to spin up. :-)
Trying to boot from one will have it spin up, at least that was
the default with my DEC RZ23L etc. disks.

The setting for spin-up is not controlled by a jumper, but located
on a vendor specific SCSI mode page, which can be changed with 
Ultrix rzdisk (IIRC). Jumpers for the SCSI ID should be somewhere
on the disks' PCBs.

Bye
Michael



