The drive is old enough not to be worth the bother, unless you are
really desparate, or you just want to tinker.

If it's a proto drive, it may have early early firmware that makes it
unreliable to use.  No way to tell.  DEC often contracted with drive
makers for largely off-the-shelf drives with firmware containing the
DEC brand.  The drive is old enough to have been used in a VAX or
DECstation setup, where the convention is for the computer to send a
SCSI spinup command to the drive, unlike most Intel boxes where the
usual rule is for a drive to spin up when powered up... Ben Myers

