In article <396932A9.41A0800D@mailbox.bellatlantic.net>, lhklik@mailbox.bellatlantic.net writes:
:Is it possible to overclock an 200 Mhz CPU to 233 Mhz?

  Sure.  (You didn't ask if it would still work, of course. :-)

  [If you are already aware of the following, no offense is intended.]

  It is common practice for semiconductor that won't run at the higher clock
  to be used at a lower clock -- parts that will run at higher clock rates 
  can be more valuable than parts that run only at lower clock rates, but
  parts that run only at the lower speeds are still useful for specific
  applications.  This process is known as "speed binning", or often just
  "binning".  (Though if there is a surfeit of faster parts, you may well 
  see these parts "idling along" at lower speeds.)

  Like all over-clocking, sometimes the particular chip you have has the 
  necessary margin (it "binned faster"), and sometimes it doesn't.

  In this case, I am not aware of an EV4 21064 at 233 MHz, only EV45 21064A 
  and later.  The EV4 bin topped out at 200 MHz, while the EV45 binned out 
  in the range of 233 to 300 MHz.  (If you actually have an EV45, you have
  a somewhat better chance of making it up to 233 MHz, of course.)

  The short answer: I and others here do not know (and really cannot know)
  if you will be able to successfully overclock this particular part.
  Nor what, if any, reduction in the expected lifetime the part might see.
  (Toasting it could thus be thought of as an excessively short lifetime. :-)

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




