Basically it works this way.

IRAN and IRAQ are most probably on the denied parties list.

That means that hardware and software cannot be sold to that country without
approvals from the highest levels of US Government.

It also means that US Companies and thier employees cannot work on that
hardware or software without approvals from the highest levels of the US
Government.

The penalties for breaking these laws knowingly or unknowingly is extremely
expensive.

At DIGITAL, we sold a system to someone. That system, unbeknownenst to
DIGITAL went all over the world and then wound up in a denied parties
country. The state department exacted an extremely expensive fine in the
10's of millions of dollars upon DIGITAL and also had DIGITAL impliment a
annual training program for each and every one of the then 120,000 employees
worldwide on export awareness training.

Do not mess around with a denied parties country, and do not ever get
involved and claim to look the other way. As far as the state department is
concerned, once you're involved, you're in it up past your eyeballs, no
matter how innocent you claim to be.

Check with the state department and know your rights.


