for the record, here is some info i have painfully gleaned,
after 6 months or so of daily Armada 6500 wrangling
(fwiw, for the price, ~800$US used without DVD, i still think
 it is a solid *buy*):

1. The HiNote 2000 and Armada 6500 are *almost* identical but
   not exactly.  The expansion interface is electrically different
   though mechanically the same.  The HiNote 2000 port replicator,
   power supply, and battery WILL work with the 6500.  BUT the
   HiNote 2000 multimedia expansion unit WILL NOT.

2. The DVD drive on my Armada 6500 would not work under Win98
   1st edition no matter how much control panel fiddling i did.

   The key is there should be a 'DMA' checkbox in the control
   panel system settings for the CDROM device.  This magic
   checkbox was nonexistent in WIn98-1e, but magically appeared
   after I installed Win98-2e on a whim.  Thereafter, once the
   checkbox is checked, presto, the DVD drive plays movies!
   (after of course installing a player, like SoftDVD etc)

   The PCMCIA DVD MPEG accessory card is evidently not
   *required* to play DVD movies.  This confused me for a while.
   I still am not sure what the MPEG card is exactly for.
   Perhaps it adds a hardware decoder vs the software decoder?
   I note that DVD movies will show an occasional frame drop or
   stutter on my Armada 6500 PII366.  I have never tried the
   MPEG card to see if it makes a difference.  It is not that
   objectionable, to me.

3. The ESS Maestro 2E sound chip used on the Armada 6500
   may be noisy and glitchy.  Some (most?) units apparently work fine.
   Mine never has, from Win95 through to Win982e and various
   versions of the drivers etc.

   My 6500 sound playback will break into an annoying
   aliasing whine at random intervals, and one channel will cut
   out at random intervals.  Doesnt matter if the sound source
   is wav midi or mp3.

   Note that the drivers on the ESS website are useless,
   as the manufacturer is the one who develops the driver
   software.  The ESS website drivers are only engineering
   application examples, not real drivers.  Compaq's drivers
   have not been updated since mid 99.  Some have advised on
   using Dell's ESS drivers, which do in fact work on the
   Armada 6500 (I tried it), but I found no difference in the
   sound quality.

   (Alas this is all particularly annoying to me as
   the Reality synth and the PropellerHeads Rebirth synth are
   my favorite laptop apps! sigh.  it must be karma ...)



