In Article <82qqlso0dhflg0jqd8dvnaf2f9g2v5l4fj@4ax.com>
JMan <jman@urlgray.com> writes:
>I'm trying to attach an external SCSI drive to a VS3100 using the
>68pin SCSI connector on the back and I'm not having much luck.
>
>The drive I'm trying this with is a 2.0G Seagate with a 68pin
>connector. If this drive is connected the entire SCSI "B" bus goes
>down - no devices are found. I know the cable I have is OK because I
>can plug the terminator into the end of the cable and all internal
>devices work OK. I've tried SCSI TERM on/off, TP power from bus and
>drive settings on the disk drive and the results are the same.
>
>I suspect that despite it being a 68pin connector this is not a SCSI 2
>Wide bus - all of the internal devices have 50pin SCSI interfaces. I
>have an external TLZ06 or 07 (4mm DAT DDS1 drive) with a centronics
>50pin connector that does work plugged into this SCSI port.

You suspect correctly.  DEC used the 68 pin connector, but the bus is a
standard narrow SCSI-2 one.  It may be fast (10 MB/s) SCSI-2, but I am
not certain of that.  If I remember right, the pin-put is not the same
as currently used on 68 pin connectors.  You should be able to attach
your drive to the external bus if you use a 68 to 50 pin adapter and
terminate the high bits of the drive.

Joe Heimann

