> just got a quote from Compaq that will run roughly $9k!  It's providing
> a RAID5, 30Gb solution using all of their hardware.  Are there
> alternatives?

Well, you could likely choose to pay EMC around twice as much...

Or you could tell the high-end RAID-box suppliers to go to hell with their
price structures and look at alternative options.  I don't know whether
Linux yet supports software mirroring (let alone RAID-5), but a mirrored
pair of 30 GB IDE drives (if you can use IDE drives with a 4100) should cost
you no more than $400 and provide decent performance.  If you place the
drives on separate IDE busses, buy drives that support disconnected
operation, and can find IDE drivers that will themselves take advantage of
disconnected operation, they should be performance-competitive with
comparable SCSI drives, which would run you closer to $1000 for a pair -
still a major bargain compared with the Compaq option, though.

Or buy an inexpensive internal RAID controller to run a mirrored pair of IDE
or SCSI disks.  Or buy one of the recent spate of external RAID boxes that
use IDE drives internally but present a SCSI interface to the host (the box
will cost around the same as a similar external SCSI-drive box, but the
drives themselves will be less expensive):  again, major bargains compared
to $9K.

Such solutions won't give you things like auto-dial-up support service when
a drive fails, but at least some support hot-spare disks that will
automatically get used in such a case (maintaining the desired redundancy
such that even a second disk failure - as long as it waits until the spare
has been brought into service - won't lose your data).

Just check that whatever you're buying comes from a source with an
established good reputation:  you may pay a few dollars more that way, but
if you care enough about your data to use RAID in the first place it's worth
it.

- bill

